WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:09.360 align:start position:19%
What if I told you the greatest library
of the ancient world did not burn down

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:14.639 align:start position:19%
in one dramatic night of flames, but
died slowly, quietly, while almost

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:19.039 align:start position:19%
nobody noticed. Hundreds of thousands of
scrolls. The collected knowledge of

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:24.240 align:start position:19%
Greece, Egypt, and Babylon gathered in
one place for the first time in history.

00:00:21.279 --> 00:00:28.880 align:start position:19%
And today, almost all of it is gone. The
library of Alexandria. You have heard

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:33.600 align:start position:19%
the legend. But what we actually lost
and how we lost it is a story nobody

00:00:31.359 --> 00:00:39.200 align:start position:19%
tells you right. So here's my question
before we start. What do you think

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:43.360 align:start position:19%
happened to it? Fire? War? Something
else? Drop your guess in the comments

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:47.120 align:start position:19%
because the real answer will surprise
you. To feel the scale of this loss, you

00:00:45.840 --> 00:00:52.239 align:start position:19%
have to understand what the tomies
built. Around 300 BC, Tommy the one of

00:00:50.399 --> 00:00:57.199 align:start position:19%
Alexander the Great's generals took over
Egypt. And he had a wild dream to

00:00:55.039 --> 00:01:01.680 align:start position:19%
collect every book in the world. every
book. He built the musam, literally a

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:05.439 align:start position:19%
temple to the muses, the goddess of
knowledge, part university, part

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:10.159 align:start position:19%
research institute, where scholars would
paid a salary to do nothing but think.

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:14.479 align:start position:19%
And attached to it, the library, the
chief librarian answered only to the

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:18.159 align:start position:19%
king. This was not a dusty archive. This
was humanity's first attempt at

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:21.920 align:start position:19%
gathering all knowledge in one place.
Imagine someone today saying, "We're

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:26.479 align:start position:19%
going to download the entire internet,
print it, and keep it in one building."

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:30.240 align:start position:19%
That is what Alexandria was.
Now, here is the part that blows my

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:34.079 align:start position:19%
mind. How they got the scrolls.
Alexandria was the busiest port in the

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:37.680 align:start position:19%
Mediterranean. And the tomies passed a
law. Every ship that entered the harbor

00:01:35.920 --> 00:01:42.240 align:start position:19%
had to hand over his books. The
librarians would copy the scrolls and

00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:45.920 align:start position:19%
keep the originals. The ship got the
copy. One story recorded by the

00:01:44.079 --> 00:01:51.200 align:start position:19%
physician Galen says, "Tlamine the third
borrowed Athens official state copies of

00:01:47.759 --> 00:01:55.759 align:start position:19%
the great tragedies. Escilis, Sophocles,
Europe. He put down a fortune of silver

00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:59.840 align:start position:19%
as a deposit and then kept the originals
and sent back the copies, telling Athens

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:03.280 align:start position:19%
to keep the money. He stole the master
copies of the greatest plays ever

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:09.599 align:start position:19%
written. Think of it as the ancient
version of download now, apologize

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:14.879 align:start position:19%
later. So, what was actually inside? The
numbers vary. Ancient sources claim

00:02:11.599 --> 00:02:18.640 align:start position:19%
anywhere from 40,000 to 400,000 scrolls.
Even the low end is staggering for the

00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:23.040 align:start position:19%
ancient world. And it was not just
storage. This was where Uklid wrote the

00:02:20.959 --> 00:02:26.800 align:start position:19%
elements. the geometry textbook your
grandparents studied. Eratosines, the

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:32.000 align:start position:19%
chief librarian, measured the
circumference of the earth with a stick,

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:37.840 align:start position:19%
a well, and shadows, and got it right
within 2%. 2,200 years before

00:02:35.280 --> 00:02:42.879 align:start position:19%
satellites, Herapyis performed the first
systematic dissections of the human body

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:47.200 align:start position:19%
here and mapped the brain. Calamakus,
another librarian, invented the library

00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:51.519 align:start position:19%
catalog, the Pinnix,
120 volumes listing every work and its

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:55.599 align:start position:19%
author. the first bibliography in
history. Think about that for a second.

00:02:54.080 --> 00:03:00.400 align:start position:19%
The search engine was invented in
Alexandria 2,000 years before Google and

00:02:58.879 --> 00:03:05.040 align:start position:19%
the people running it were the greatest
minds alive. Let me give you my favorite

00:03:03.519 --> 00:03:10.800 align:start position:19%
Erosines.
Around 240 BC, he heard that in the city

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:16.720 align:start position:19%
of Sen at noon on the summer solstice,
the sun shone straight down a well. No

00:03:13.519 --> 00:03:22.000 align:start position:19%
shadow at all. But in Alexandria on the
same day, a stick did cast a shadow.

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:26.000 align:start position:19%
From that one difference, he calculated
the angle of the shadow, estimated the

00:03:24.159 --> 00:03:32.080 align:start position:19%
distance between the two cities, and
computed the Earth's circumference,

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:38.959 align:start position:19%
about 39,000 km. The real number, just
over 40,000. He was off by less than 2%

00:03:35.760 --> 00:03:44.799 align:start position:19%
with a stick and a well. That to me is
the library of Alexandria in one story.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:49.239 align:start position:19%
Curiosity turned into mathematics turned
into truth about the entire planet.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:59.040 align:start position:19%
A few years ago, a student asked me what
the library of Alexandria would be

00:03:55.840 --> 00:04:04.080 align:start position:19%
today. And I said, "It already exists.
It is Wikipedia. It is the internet

00:04:01.439 --> 00:04:08.239 align:start position:19%
archive. It is every open library you
have ever used." But here's the

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:13.040 align:start position:19%
difference. We think digital means
forever. The Alexandrians thought

00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:16.560 align:start position:19%
papyrus and stone meant forever, too.
What fascinates me about this is that

00:04:14.799 --> 00:04:21.359 align:start position:19%
the people of Alexandria were so
confident their knowledge was safe and

00:04:18.799 --> 00:04:26.479 align:start position:19%
they were wrong. If stories about the
fragile human side of history are your

00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:31.120 align:start position:19%
thing, subscribe to Ink Time. One video
a week that makes you rethink the past

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:35.759 align:start position:19%
and stay with me because the part about
the fire, almost everything you know

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:40.320 align:start position:19%
about it is wrong.
Okay, the fire. You have seen the movie

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:45.120 align:start position:19%
version. Julius Caesar torches the
harbor in 48 BC and the Great Library

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:49.280 align:start position:19%
goes up in flames. Here's what actually
happened. Caesar was besieged in

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:53.600 align:start position:19%
Alexandria and set fire to his own ships
to block the enemy fleet. The fire

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:59.520 align:start position:19%
spread to warehouses on the docks. The
Roman writer Cynica says 40,000 scrolls

00:04:56.479 --> 00:05:03.280 align:start position:19%
burned, but and this is crucial, those
were most likely books stored in the

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:08.800 align:start position:19%
harbor warehouses, duplicates waiting to
be cataloged. The main library up in the

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:14.000 align:start position:19%
royal quarter probably survived. One
fire 48 BC did not destroy the library

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:19.919 align:start position:19%
of Alexandria. The legend of the single
great fire, it is mostly a myth. The

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:25.199 align:start position:19%
truth is slower and sadder cuz the
library did not die in a night. It died

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:29.840 align:start position:19%
over centuries. The later Tommy kings
cared more about palace politics than

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:34.160 align:start position:19%
scholars. Budgets were cut and the best
minds left for Rome and elsewhere. By

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:39.360 align:start position:19%
the time of the Roman Emperor Aurelion
in the 270s AD, his troops destroyed the

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:43.759 align:start position:19%
Brucian, the royal quarter where the
library stood. The smaller daughter

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:49.360 align:start position:19%
library in the Serapium temple survived
longer until 391 AD when a mob acting

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:55.360 align:start position:19%
under imperial orders tore the temple
down. No single villain, no single fire,

00:05:52.960 --> 00:06:00.240 align:start position:19%
just neglect, war, riots, and budget
cuts. The most boring apocalypse

00:05:57.680 --> 00:06:04.960 align:start position:19%
imaginable. What fascinates me is how
ordinary it all was. Knowledge does not

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:10.560 align:start position:19%
usually die screaming. It dies from
nobody bothering to save it. So, what do

00:06:08.000 --> 00:06:15.280 align:start position:19%
we actually lose? This is the part that
hurts. Of the roughly 1,200 known

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:20.560 align:start position:19%
ancient Greek authors, we have complete
works from only a handful. Escilis wrote

00:06:17.759 --> 00:06:27.520 align:start position:19%
around 90 plays, seven survive.
Sophocles wrote about 120. Seven

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:31.039 align:start position:19%
survive. Uripides 90 plays, 19 survive.
Safo, the most famous poet of the

00:06:29.199 --> 00:06:35.520 align:start position:19%
ancient world, survives only in
fragments, a single complete poem.

00:06:33.600 --> 00:06:39.919 align:start position:19%
Entire schools of philosophy, history,
scientific treatises, we know them only

00:06:37.600 --> 00:06:44.080 align:start position:19%
as titles in other people's footnotes.
Now, think about this. If even one more

00:06:42.160 --> 00:06:47.680 align:start position:19%
warehouse of scrolls has survived, our
picture of the ancient world could be

00:06:45.440 --> 00:06:52.479 align:start position:19%
completely different. The library's real
tragedy is not what burned. It is what

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:56.319 align:start position:19%
faded silently, title by title, until
nobody remembered what was missing.

00:06:54.800 --> 00:07:00.000 align:start position:19%
2,000 years later, the Library of
Alexandria is still teaching us one

00:06:57.919 --> 00:07:05.199 align:start position:19%
lesson. Civilization is not a one-way
road. Knowledge is not automatically

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:09.360 align:start position:19%
safe. Not on papyrus, not on servers,
not anywhere. Every generation has to

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:13.039 align:start position:19%
choose to keep it. The Alexandrians
collected the world's knowledge once. We

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:17.120 align:start position:19%
did it again with the internet. The
question is, will we do better than they

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:21.199 align:start position:19%
did? If this story gave you chills,
subscribe to Ink Time and tell me in the

00:07:19.280 --> 00:07:25.840 align:start position:19%
comments, if you could save one lost
work from Alexandria, what would it be?

